WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.280
Cells do not just end up a size.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.340
Growing cells hold their size by linking growth to division.

3
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.540
A fission yeast cell divides only once it reaches a set size.

4
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.140
Born small, it grows more first.

5
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.340
Born large, it grows less.

6
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.740
That matters, because size sets how much a cell can make and how much room each part

7
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.920
gets. The nucleus, for one, grows with the cell.

8
00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.120
So how does a cell know its size?

9
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:31.513
One way is dilution.

10
00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:36.573
In budding yeast, every newborn cell gets about the same amount of a brake on

11
00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:39.633
division, a protein called Whi5.

12
00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:42.593
As the cell grows, the brake is diluted.

13
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:47.273
A big cell dilutes it sooner and is more likely to commit to dividing.

14
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:50.713
A cell born small waits, and grows more first.

15
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:53.957
Size also sets what a cell can make.

16
00:00:54.757 --> 00:00:59.597
To keep its molecules at the same concentration, a growing cell must make more RNA

17
00:00:59.597 --> 00:01:04.057
and protein. Most cells do, roughly in step with volume.

18
00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:09.897
That has a limit. Budding yeast held so they grow without dividing get many times

19
00:01:09.897 --> 00:01:12.197
bigger, and synthesis falls behind.

20
00:01:13.157 --> 00:01:14.917
Their cytoplasm becomes diluted.

21
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.140
The parts scale too, but their shares depend on the cell.

22
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.180
A little more than a quarter of one hamster cell line is nucleus.

23
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.960
In fission yeast is under one tenth.

24
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.760
There, the nucleus grows with the cell across a thirty-five-fold range of sizes, and

25
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.660
a sixteen-fold change in DNA leaves the ratio unchanged.

26
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:38.613
Plant cells take a short cut.

27
00:01:39.493 --> 00:01:44.773
In Arabidopsis root epidermis, cells grew about fourteen-fold in volume, but the

28
00:01:44.773 --> 00:01:48.993
space between vacuole and cell boundary grew only about two to threefold.

29
00:01:49.953 --> 00:01:54.093
Most new volume is vacuole, and the wall has to loosen to let the cell stretch.

30
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:57.407
Geometry adds a cost to being big.

31
00:01:58.307 --> 00:02:00.347
Everything a cell takes in crosses its surface.

32
00:02:01.247 --> 00:02:03.607
Everything it uses is spread through its volume.

33
00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:09.167
Double a sphere's radius, and its area rises fourfold while its volume rises

34
00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:14.707
eightfold. With fixed uptake per area and fixed demand per volume, supply falls

35
00:02:14.707 --> 00:02:16.187
behind past some radius.

36
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.620
Keep the order. Cells set their size by linking growth to division.

37
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.740
Size then sets what they can make and how their parts share the room.

38
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.880
Surface and volume add a cost, not a universal maximum.
